1 Introduction. 
In this paper, we compare the incentives for information acquisition between the all-pay auction and the first-price auction, and between the war of attrition (second-price all-pay auction) and the second-price auction. As well-known models of contests, the all-pay auction (APA) and the war of attrition (WOA) involve unconditional investments due to their "all-pay rule". The first-price auction (FPA) and second-price auction (SPA), on the other hand, are competitions with conditional investments corresponding to the all-pay auction and war of attrition, respectively.

Persico (2000) shows that players with affiliated values have stronger incentives to acquire information about their value in the FPA than the SPA. When a player acquires information, her signal is more correlated with her value and hence, the opponent's signal due to the affiliation. This implies that her bid is more correlated with the opponent's bid, and her winning bid must be just above the opponent's bid. The information acquisition effectively reduces the difference between the winning bid and the second-highest bid in the FPA, i.e., the "money left on the table". Since in the SPA, the winner pays the second-highest bid, there is no money left on the table. Hence, players have greater incentives to acquire information in the FPA than the SPA.

Following the same method given by Persico (2000), we show that players in the FPA have stronger incentives for information acquisition than the APA, and stronger such incentives in the SPA than the WOA. However, the "money left on the table" intuition no longer applies here because the winner pays her own bid in both the FPA and the APA (and second-highest bid in both the SPA and the WOA). It is, in fact, the all-pay rule that discourages the players from correlating their bids with the opponent. In the AOA, correlation of bids results in not only close wins (hence, no money left on the table), but also close losses that are costly. This reasoning implies that information about the object is less valuable in the APA than the FPA. The same reasoning applies to interpret the finding that players have weaker incentives to correlate their bids in the WOA than the SPA: the bids are forfeited in the event of losing the WOA rather than the SPA.

We show that the incentives are stronger in the APA than the WOA, which is due to the "money left on the table" effect, similar to the comparison between the FPA and the SPA in Persico (2000). In the event of winning the auctions, players pay their bid in the APA, but second-highest bid the WOA. The "all-pay rule" effect plays no role in this comparison because they pay their bids in both mechanisms in the event of losing the auctions.

When comparing the incentives between the FPA and the WOA, both the "money left on the table" effect and the "all-pay rule" effect play a role. The combination of the two effects result in a stronger incentives in the FPA than the WOA. In particular, players pay the second-highest bid in the event of winning the WOA but their own bid in the FPA, hence the "money left on the table" effect predicts stronger incentives in the FPA. Furthermore, they pay their own bids in the event of losing the WOA but nothing in the FPA, hence the "all-pay rule" effect again predicts stronger incentives in the FPA.

The two effects work against each other in comparing the incentives between the APA and the SPA. The ranking of the incentives between the two is thus ambiguous. Here, the "money left on the table" effect predicts stronger incentives in the APA than the SPA, whereas the "allpay rule" effect predicts the opposite. Our results, together with Persico (2000)'s finding that the incentives are stronger in the FPA than the SPA, are summarized in Figure 1.
